Question: BERT 在该任务上的表现如何？ Answer: 24.2. BERT: BERT has shown an outstanding performance in NER-like tasks, improving the start-of-the-art results on several benchmarks. It also outperforms the state-of-the-art model in the task of named entity recognition in the Spanish corpus of medical real narratives annotated with manual annotations. In addition, BERT has been used to compare the performance of other models in the task of sentiment analysis and in the task of question answering. BERT has also been used to train a model that can generate human-like responses to questions. Answer: BERT has shown an outstanding performance in NER-like tasks, improving the start-of-the-art results on several benchmarks. It also outperforms the state-of-the-art model in the task of named entity recognition in the Spanish corpus of medical real narratives annotated with manual annotations. In addition, BERT has been used to compare the performance of other models in the task of sentiment analysis and in the task of question answering. BERT has also been used to train a model that can generate human-like responses to questions. Therefore, BERT is a powerful model that can be used in a wide range of NLP tasks. 24.3. Experimental design: In this experiment, we set up the experiments to compare the performance of BERT with the state-of-the-art models on several benchmarks. We use the Spanish corpus of medical real narratives annotated with manual annotations to evaluate the performance of BERT in the task of named entity recognition. We also use the MedDAN corpus to evaluate the performance of BERT in the task of sentiment analysis. We use the CoNLL-2003 dataset to evaluate the performance of BERT in the task of question answering. We also use the NER-84 corpus server to evaluate the performance of BERT in the task of named entity recognition. The experiments were run on a 64-core server with operating system Ubuntu 16.04 and 16GB of RAM. The maximum batch size was 24, and the maximum sequence length was 512 tokens. The model was trained for 12 epochs. In this setting, each epoch pass a full through all the data. 24.4. Conclusion: BERT is a pre-trained model that can be fine-tuned for a wide range of NLP tasks. BERT has shown an outstanding performance in NER-like tasks, improving the start-of-the-art results on several benchmarks. It also outperforms the state-of-the
