```python
with most at most (words: 6, 7) the total number of actions is in O(√N) (e.g. 20K ∗ 1.2%) . 
To reach this number, these several existing solutions focus on either sparsely-given, but with 
very small vocabularies where the action space is constrained or in small-scale games with 
works rely on using predetermined sets of admissible actions (He et al., 2015; Tesler et al., 2014) 
or on using a separate, admissible common action space (Zahavy et al., 2015) or by 
generating a separate set of admissible actions eliminating actions (Zahavy et al., 2010) or by 
completing them in a full, unconstrained action space of natural language that can systematically 
generate any possible action (Zahavy et al., 2016). 
To address this challenge, we propose to use the idea behind the recently proposed Go-Explore 
method (Zahavy et al., 2016) to generate a large number of admissible actions in an imitation learning 
framework. The effectiveness of the proposed method is demonstrated in several games, in which 
the number of actions is in O(√N) (e.g. 20K ∗ 1.2%) and the number of words is in O(N) (e.g. 20K ∗ 1.2%) . 
In addition, the proposed method is shown to be more robust than the other methods in the 
environment, as the element of this set is chosen as the action for that observation. However, in 
environments with a large number of actions, the proposed method may not be able to generate 
actions that are optimal in terms of efficiency. 
